WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.502
ColPali's significant memory footprint

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:04.571
can be addressed with optimization techniques.

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:07.073
but it still doesn't scale well since

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:09.275
you can't use it with HNSW search.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:12.779
Now, let's look at an approach called MUVERA,

00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:15.982
which unlocks HNSW search for multi-vectors

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:18.985
and addresses ColPali's fine-grained measure flaw.

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:21.521
Let's go.

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:23.223
The idea behind MUVERA

00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:26.226
is to convert variable length sequences of vectors

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:28.428
into fixed dimensional embeddings,

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:31.231
making it possible to use the same approximate methods

00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:33.933
like we use for regular dense vector search.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.303
ColPali can't be used with HNSW search

00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:40.507
because there is no way to build the HNSW index

00:00:40.507 --> 00:00:43.109
that powers approximate nearest neighbor search

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:46.079
for asymmetric similarity measure like MaxSim.

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:49.783
As a result, ColPali requires a brute force search.

00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:52.018
technique that takes linear time.

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:55.422
If you want to search a million documents with ColPali,

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:58.124
you need to calculate 1 million max sims.

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:02.095
MUVERA approaches that differently by converting sequences of vectors

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:05.198
into highly dimensional single vector embeddings.

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:08.034
That enables the use of HNSW.

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:11.337
HNSW instead scales in logarithmic time,

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:15.041
scaling easily to millions or billions of documents.

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:19.345
It makes it more practical than original multi-vector representations.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.882
Let's review the steps required to implement that technique.

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:26.186
The MUVERA algorithm is based on a few steps.

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:29.556
First of all, it aims to perform a clustering algorithm

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:31.257
on the multi-vector embeddings.

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:35.528
The MUVERA paper suggests using locality sensitive hashing method,

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:38.698
namely SimHash. Assume each of our tokens

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:42.602
is mapped to a vector space in our case two-dimensional.

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:45.772
SimHash will then generate some random hyperplanes

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:48.074
splitting that space into two parts.

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.477
Each vector will end up on one

00:01:50.477 --> 00:01:52.745
of the sides as defined by the hyperplane.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:56.816
However, one hyperplane is just not enough.

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:59.319
SimHash will generate K hyperplanes,

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:02.889
dividing the space into 2 to the power of K buckets.

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:06.493
We can assign binary strings to each of these buckets

00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:09.496
by using either zero or one to indicate the fact

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:12.098
of being on either side of the corresponding hyperplane.

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:15.201
The length of the bucket ID will be exactly K

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:17.403
in terms of the bits used.

00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:19.706
In our case, it's just two,

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:21.808
meaning there are exactly four different buckets,

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:25.211
as that's how many values you can represent using two bits.

00:02:26.012 --> 00:02:29.215
After assigning buckets to each of the input token embeddings,

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:32.418
we group the terms belonging to the same cluster together.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:36.723
SimHash aims to reduce the variable length of the sequences

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:40.326
by finding some representative vectors for each of the buckets.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.897
Document and query processing is slightly different.

00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:47.467
In the case of documents, SimHash will take an average

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:50.837
of all the token vectors belonging to a particular cluster.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.840
Since some of the buckets might be empty,

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.209
it will also fill them with the

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:58.545
value assigned to the closest non-empty cluster

00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:00.547
in terms of the Hamming distance.

00:03:00.547 --> 00:03:02.415
Just to give you an intuition, the

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:04.250
Hamming distance will be equal to one

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.253
between the cluster IDs that differ

00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:10.790
by just one bit. At the very end of that process,

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:14.093
each of the clusters will have a single vector representing it.

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:17.497
That means no matter how many input vectors you had,

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:19.799
you will always end up with two

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:22.101
to the power of k representative vectors.

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:24.804
Query processing is slightly different.

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:26.806
Instead of taking an average, you sum

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:28.508
all the vectors in the same cluster.

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:32.645
This means that the resulting vectors will have larger magnitudes

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:35.048
for clusters with more vectors assigned.

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:38.952
The idea is that it preserves the natural distribution of query terms,

00:03:38.952 --> 00:03:41.521
which can be beneficial for retrieval.

00:03:41.821 --> 00:03:43.623
Unlike with documents,

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:46.226
you do not fill empty clusters for queries.

00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:48.828
Queries are typically shorter than documents,

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:51.998
so it's more likely that some clusters will be empty.

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:56.436
filling them could introduce noise and distort the representation,

00:03:56.536 --> 00:04:00.240
as each term would contribute to the dot product multiple times.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.643
So, if there were no tokens in a particular cluster,

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:06.045
it will be represented as a zero vector.

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:08.915
No matter if process documents or queries,

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:11.384
you now have a single 128-dimensional vector

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:14.887
for each cluster created by the SimHash procedure.

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:18.391
128-dimensional, as it's the most typical length

00:04:18.391 --> 00:04:21.194
of a single vector in multi-vector representations.

00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:23.930
Quite often, the number of hyperplanes in the

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.266
SimHash procedure is set to at least five,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.701
giving us 32 clusters.

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:32.905
That's 4096 dimensions in total. Quite a lot.

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:35.174
The distances between the vectors are

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:37.210
the most important thing for semantic search.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:41.214
When you work with data in a very high dimensional space,

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:43.916
it becomes hard to process, but you can shrink the data

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:45.918
down without losing much information.

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:49.222
Random projection is a clever trick that projects high dimensional data

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:51.891
into a lower dimensional space

00:04:51.891 --> 00:04:54.394
using a matrix filled with random values,

00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:57.196
typically just positive or negative one.

00:04:57.597 --> 00:05:01.901
Surprisingly, this simple method keeps the distances between points nearly the same,

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:04.404
thanks to the Johnson-Lindenstrauss lemma.

00:05:05.004 --> 00:05:07.307
This lemma guarantees that if you choose

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:10.410
enough dimensions, you can reduce the data safely.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.912
So, with just a bit of random math,

00:05:13.313 --> 00:05:15.148
you preserve the shape of the data

00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:16.983
while making it much easier to handle.

00:05:17.317 --> 00:05:21.621
So, when you have a matrix with the vectors after applying SimHash,

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:25.725
you multiply it by another matrix of positive or negative ones,

00:05:26.125 --> 00:05:30.630
which has a shape of original dimension times target dimension.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.032
As a result, you reduce the

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:35.234
dimensionality of each of the cluster vectors

00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:37.503
to a target dimensionality you want to

00:05:37.503 --> 00:05:39.706
have per cluster. As simple as that.

00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:43.109
Random projection can keep the mean similarity error really low,

00:05:43.409 --> 00:05:46.612
even if you reduce the number of dimensions quite significantly.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:51.784
The chart presents what it looks like for 10000 randomly generated vectors

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:54.887
of 8096 dimensions each.

00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:59.192
It's an interesting method which might be useful not only for MUVERA

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:02.595
but also just to reduce the dimensionality of any vectors.

00:06:03.096 --> 00:06:05.598
As the chart shows, the error remains the same

00:06:05.598 --> 00:06:08.201
at 4000 dimensions, 1000,

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:11.904
and it doesn't meaningfully increase until you are close to a hundred.

00:06:12.372 --> 00:06:16.175
This means you can significantly reduce the dimensionality of your data

00:06:16.376 --> 00:06:20.980
while only introducing minimal errors in terms of relative distances.

00:06:21.381 --> 00:06:23.549
You still have multi-vector representation.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:27.453
Yet at this point, it's always a sequence of the same length.

00:06:27.854 --> 00:06:30.356
Each cluster vector has a dimensionality

00:06:30.356 --> 00:06:32.859
equal to the target dimensionality of the Random Projection.

00:06:33.192 --> 00:06:37.196
However, HNSW search needs just one vector.

00:06:37.697 --> 00:06:39.999
So we simply concatenate them all

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:42.301
in an order defined by cluster IDs.

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:46.506
At the very end, you get a long single vector representation,

00:06:46.906 --> 00:06:49.075
but you can control how long it is

00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:51.978
by modifying the parameters of Random Projection.

00:06:52.311 --> 00:06:54.881
At this point, you've probably realized

00:06:54.881 --> 00:06:57.917
that MUVERA heavily relies on random procedures.

00:06:58.418 --> 00:07:00.319
If you are not lucky enough,

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:03.156
the SimHash hyperplane may divide the space in a way

00:07:03.156 --> 00:07:05.992
so two tokens will be on different sides of it,

00:07:06.359 --> 00:07:08.060
even though they have much in common.

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:10.196
Dog is the only animal that

00:07:10.196 --> 00:07:12.298
can bark, but by a random chance,

00:07:12.665 --> 00:07:15.101
these tokens may end up in different buckets.

00:07:15.668 --> 00:07:19.172
That's why MUVERA assumes multiple repetitions of the whole process.

00:07:19.705 --> 00:07:23.810
That applies to both similarity hashing as well as random projections.

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:26.212
The number of repetitions is another

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:28.581
parameter of the process you can control.

00:07:29.182 --> 00:07:31.484
Quite often, you set it to at least 10.

00:07:32.185 --> 00:07:34.520
Once you repeat the process, you concatenate

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:37.190
all the embeddings and that's your final vector.

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:40.626
Typically, there is no need to implement that process from scratch,

00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:43.229
but you can rely on an existing implementation.

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:45.932
So, let's see how it works in action.

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:49.635
Let's configure MUVERA for ColPali embeddings in production.

00:07:50.336 --> 00:07:53.940
You will use k_sim equal to 6 to create 64 clusters.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:58.244
and random projection will compress each cluster vector

00:07:58.945 --> 00:08:02.949
from a 128 dimensional one to just 16 dimensions.

00:08:03.249 --> 00:08:05.451
The entire process will be repeated 20 times.

00:08:05.952 --> 00:08:09.956
Each repetition uses different random hyperplanes for SimHash.

00:08:10.089 --> 00:08:13.092
and different random matrices for the Random Projection,

00:08:13.593 --> 00:08:16.896
creating diverse representations that are concatenated together.

00:08:17.597 --> 00:08:21.501
This gives us 64 clusters times 16 dimensions.

00:08:22.602 --> 00:08:24.904
times 20 repetitions, which is

00:08:24.904 --> 00:08:28.307
20480 dimensions per document.

00:08:28.808 --> 00:08:31.210
It's larger than a typical dense vector,

00:08:31.711 --> 00:08:33.913
but it enables way faster retrieval

00:08:33.913 --> 00:08:36.516
compared to full multi-vector search

00:08:36.516 --> 00:08:38.518
while still maintaining high precision.

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:43.389
Please notice you explicitly set the value of the random seed.

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:46.192
Since MUVERA is based on randomness,

00:08:46.492 --> 00:08:48.494
you do it to ensure reproducibility.

00:08:48.861 --> 00:08:51.864
All the documents and queries have to be processed

00:08:51.864 --> 00:08:54.300
through the same SimHash hyperplanes

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.669
and the Random Projection matrices.

00:08:56.669 --> 00:08:59.672
Otherwise, they won't be compatible with each other.

00:09:00.273 --> 00:09:01.941
You can again make a decision

00:09:01.941 --> 00:09:03.876
whether you want to create the embeddings

00:09:04.277 --> 00:09:05.978
or just use the precomputed ones.

00:09:06.679 --> 00:09:09.181
Now let's apply MUVERA to real document retrieval.

00:09:09.782 --> 00:09:11.684
You will use the same PDF

00:09:11.684 --> 00:09:13.886
screenshot dataset like in the previous lesson.

00:09:14.687 --> 00:09:17.290
Those are pages of lecture notes,

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:20.593
each encoded with ColPali's multi-vector embeddings.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:24.830
The same helper function will handle loading this precomputed embeddings

00:09:24.931 --> 00:09:26.933
or computing them fresh if needed.

00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:30.937
This embeddings contain over a thousand token vectors per page,

00:09:31.037 --> 00:09:33.940
making them perfect candidates for MUVERA compression.

00:09:34.307 --> 00:09:37.209
Time to compress this multi-vector embeddings

00:09:37.209 --> 00:09:39.178
into fixed dimensional encodings.

00:09:39.579 --> 00:09:43.482
We'll process each document through our MUVERA instance.

00:09:44.083 --> 00:09:46.385
The process_document method

00:09:46.385 --> 00:09:48.588
handles this complete pipeline.

00:09:48.788 --> 00:09:52.191
SimHash clustering, averaging within clusters,

00:09:52.592 --> 00:09:54.794
concatenating all cluster vectors

00:09:54.794 --> 00:09:56.896
after applying Random Projections.

00:09:57.296 --> 00:09:59.498
This transforms each page's

00:09:59.999 --> 00:10:03.803
1031 variable length vectors into a single

00:10:04.203 --> 00:10:07.506
20480 dimensional FDE.

00:10:08.007 --> 00:10:09.909
Now let's prepare the search queries.

00:10:10.476 --> 00:10:13.379
We'll still use the same three queries.

00:10:14.113 --> 00:10:16.015
like we did in the previous lesson,

00:10:16.315 --> 00:10:19.352
and process them with MUVERA query pipeline.

00:10:20.252 --> 00:10:23.656
The helper function will load or create query embeddings

00:10:23.656 --> 00:10:25.658
similarly like we did in the previous lesson.

00:10:25.858 --> 00:10:30.429
Now we can process these queries through the same MUVERA process.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:35.735
However, please notice that we are calling process_query and not process document

00:10:35.735 --> 00:10:39.939
anymore because there is a slightly different way of how we approach

00:10:39.939 --> 00:10:43.142
queries in the MUVERA algorithm.

00:10:43.776 --> 00:10:46.579
Now comes the exciting part. Creating a Qdrant collection

00:10:46.579 --> 00:10:49.382
that stores both representations side by side.

00:10:49.782 --> 00:10:51.784
We'll set up two named vectors.

00:10:51.984 --> 00:10:54.587
One for the original ColPali multi-vectors

00:10:55.287 --> 00:10:58.591
with MaxSim comparison and HNSW disabled,

00:10:59.291 --> 00:11:02.561
and one for MUVERA fixed dimensional encodings

00:11:02.795 --> 00:11:05.765
as regular single vectors with HNSW enabled.

00:11:06.666 --> 00:11:10.469
This setup allows you to directly compare retrieval speed

00:11:10.469 --> 00:11:12.672
and accuracy between the two approaches.

00:11:13.472 --> 00:11:16.475
Let's insert all the document pages into our collection,

00:11:16.475 --> 00:11:19.178
providing both representations for each.

00:11:19.812 --> 00:11:23.416
This allows Qdrant to index and search using either approach.

00:11:24.316 --> 00:11:26.185
Once you upload your vectors into

00:11:26.185 --> 00:11:28.421
Qdrant they are immediately available for search.

00:11:28.721 --> 00:11:31.590
However, the HNSW graph might still not be ready

00:11:31.691 --> 00:11:34.293
and the optimizer might be building it in the background.

00:11:34.994 --> 00:11:36.996
In production you rarely care about that,

00:11:37.296 --> 00:11:38.998
but here we really want to

00:11:38.998 --> 00:11:41.000
test the approximate search for MUVERA embeddings.

00:11:41.167 --> 00:11:45.171
Checking the collection status in a loop until it becomes green

00:11:45.171 --> 00:11:47.773
is an easy way of making sure it's all done.

00:11:48.174 --> 00:11:50.042
Time for the moment of truth.

00:11:50.142 --> 00:11:52.445
Let's compare retrieval quality and speed

00:11:52.645 --> 00:11:55.448
between ColPali's original multi-vectors

00:11:55.648 --> 00:11:58.050
and MUVERA's compressed representations.

00:11:58.617 --> 00:12:00.820
We'll search with both approaches

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:02.922
and examine which documents they retrieve.

00:12:03.622 --> 00:12:06.826
MUVERA should provide significantly faster search

00:12:06.826 --> 00:12:08.794
while maintaining high precision.

00:12:09.662 --> 00:12:13.165
We'll define three simple helper functions to make our research experiment

00:12:13.165 --> 00:12:15.468
clear and easy to follow.

00:12:16.268 --> 00:12:19.739
Let's start with one for ColPali vectors.

00:12:20.239 --> 00:12:24.610
Each of the helper functions will return results and timing.

00:12:25.111 --> 00:12:29.815
Then a comparison function will calculate precision and speed up metrics.

00:12:30.116 --> 00:12:32.918
This separation makes it easy to run each search

00:12:33.219 --> 00:12:35.621
independently and understand what's happening.

00:12:36.021 --> 00:12:38.424
The next helper function will perform search

00:12:39.225 --> 00:12:41.127
using just the MUVERA embeddings.

00:12:41.494 --> 00:12:45.998
Let's test our first query by searching with both methods independently.

00:12:46.999 --> 00:12:51.704
We'll start with ColPali's original multi-vector search, which is our baseline,

00:12:52.004 --> 00:12:55.407
and then run the same query through MUVERA's compressed vector,

00:12:55.608 --> 00:12:58.010
and finally compare the results to see

00:12:58.177 --> 00:13:00.479
the precision and speedup metrics.

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:03.182
As you can see, MUVERA was

00:13:04.183 --> 00:13:06.385
18 times faster in average,

00:13:07.186 --> 00:13:10.055
which makes it a good candidate for improving the

00:13:10.256 --> 00:13:12.958
speed of multi-vector search.

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:16.262
Here is how the ColPali

00:13:16.962 --> 00:13:18.964
retrieved documents look like.

00:13:22.568 --> 00:13:25.037
And here is what MUVERA was able to retrieve.

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:29.975
We are having a look at it

00:13:29.975 --> 00:13:31.677
because the precision reported for that query

00:13:31.677 --> 00:13:33.646
was only 40%.

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:38.450
Now let's test the second query following the same pattern.

00:13:39.185 --> 00:13:41.787
MUVERA is still significantly faster

00:13:41.987 --> 00:13:44.356
and the precision is relatively low.

00:13:44.757 --> 00:13:48.160
If you are interested in seeing the individual results,

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.162
they are just displayed here.

00:13:53.165 --> 00:13:55.467
Finally, let's test the third query

00:13:55.467 --> 00:13:57.469
to complete our comparison.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:01.440
Even though MUVERA is again 18 times faster

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:03.843
than original ColPali embeddings.

00:14:04.243 --> 00:14:06.445
the precision of 0%

00:14:06.445 --> 00:14:09.014
doesn't make it any interesting

00:14:09.014 --> 00:14:11.717
if you really care about the quality of search.

00:14:13.419 --> 00:14:15.721
Here is how the search results look like.

00:14:16.388 --> 00:14:19.992
And you can clearly see that MUVERA wasn't really able

00:14:19.992 --> 00:14:23.696
to find the best matches for our query.

00:14:24.196 --> 00:14:27.199
Let's calculate the average performance across all queries

00:14:27.199 --> 00:14:30.202
to see the overall impact of MUVERA compression.

00:14:30.703 --> 00:14:34.506
In average, MUVERA is 17 times faster

00:14:34.506 --> 00:14:37.309
than original multi-vector representations.

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:40.713
However, there is an enormous precision loss

00:14:40.713 --> 00:14:42.915
compared to the original ColPali embeddings.

00:14:43.148 --> 00:14:45.384
In production, the optimal approach combines

00:14:45.384 --> 00:14:47.953
both methods in a single efficient query.

00:14:48.654 --> 00:14:51.757
Use MUVERA for fast initial retrieval of candidate documents,

00:14:51.957 --> 00:14:56.061
and then rerank those same documents with the original ColPali multi-vectors

00:14:56.662 --> 00:14:58.364
for maximum accuracy.

00:14:58.364 --> 00:15:00.766
Let's create another helper function.

00:15:01.367 --> 00:15:04.870
Qdrant's prefetch mechanism lets you implement

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:08.173
this entire pipeline in one API call.

00:15:08.574 --> 00:15:12.144
MUVERA retrieves more candidates than expected.

00:15:12.144 --> 00:15:14.446
exactly 10 times more,

00:15:14.647 --> 00:15:16.949
and then ColPali ranks

00:15:16.949 --> 00:15:18.651
to get exactly N results.

00:15:18.951 --> 00:15:21.620
This gives you the speed of single vector search

00:15:21.620 --> 00:15:23.923
with the precision of late interaction models,

00:15:24.623 --> 00:15:26.625
as running max sim on a small

00:15:26.625 --> 00:15:28.627
subset is going to be fast enough.

00:15:28.827 --> 00:15:32.131
It does not make much sense to run prefetch with MUVERA

00:15:32.131 --> 00:15:34.033
and get exactly that many documents as

00:15:34.033 --> 00:15:35.935
we want to present to the user.

00:15:36.535 --> 00:15:38.637
If we use ColPali for reranking,

00:15:38.637 --> 00:15:41.407
then it can only reorder the documents it was given.

00:15:41.607 --> 00:15:44.710
So, if MUVERA didn't return the best matches possible,

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:47.913
it will just find an order for this suboptimal set.

00:15:48.447 --> 00:15:51.150
Let's run the same set of tests

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:55.454
using ColPali multi-vectors and two-stage retrieval

00:15:55.454 --> 00:15:58.057
with MUVERA and ColPali reranking.

00:15:58.057 --> 00:16:00.259
We'll start with the first query.

00:16:00.926 --> 00:16:04.229
You can definitely see that the Speedup we got

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:07.232
is not that spectacular.

00:16:07.333 --> 00:16:09.635
However, the Precision is slightly better

00:16:09.835 --> 00:16:11.937
because it's currently at 80%.

00:16:12.304 --> 00:16:14.506
for the coffee mug query.

00:16:15.107 --> 00:16:17.409
The ColPali results have not changed.

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:20.312
However, we can expect two-stage retrieval to

00:16:20.813 --> 00:16:22.614
get a bit better results.

00:16:23.115 --> 00:16:26.719
And indeed, it did its job a little bit better.

00:16:26.986 --> 00:16:28.988
Let's test the second query.

00:16:29.188 --> 00:16:32.491
You are getting better results with every single attempt.

00:16:32.591 --> 00:16:35.461
You should expect the speed up to be quite consistent,

00:16:35.561 --> 00:16:38.163
but this time we are getting an ideal precision.

00:16:38.364 --> 00:16:41.967
That means we are getting the same exact set of results

00:16:42.067 --> 00:16:46.472
for both ColPali embeddings and two-stage MUVERA plus ColPali reranking.

00:16:47.039 --> 00:16:49.842
There's one remaining query that we still want to test.

00:16:50.743 --> 00:16:54.847
Unfortunately, this time MUVERA two-stage retrieval was not able to find

00:16:54.947 --> 00:16:56.949
any meaningful result.

00:16:57.416 --> 00:17:00.319
And indeed it's keep returning some random slides

00:17:00.719 --> 00:17:03.489
that have nothing in common with the query provided.

00:17:03.989 --> 00:17:06.792
However, we can control the quality of retrieval

00:17:07.192 --> 00:17:08.994
for two-stage pipeline

00:17:09.895 --> 00:17:12.197
by increasing the oversampling rate.

00:17:12.264 --> 00:17:15.267
For example, by setting the oversampling rate to 20,

00:17:15.534 --> 00:17:19.738
so we can get 100 results to rerank with ColPali.

00:17:20.239 --> 00:17:23.742
Let's summarize the results of all the queries.

00:17:24.743 --> 00:17:28.447
As you can see, the average time spent on two-stage retrieval

00:17:28.447 --> 00:17:30.749
is still significantly lower.

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:34.553
than for the entire ColPali embeddings.

00:17:34.553 --> 00:17:37.856
However, the precision might not be acceptable

00:17:37.856 --> 00:17:40.926
if you really need the best matches possible.

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:44.063
However, we successfully demonstrated MUVERA's

00:17:44.063 --> 00:17:47.166
practical application to multimodal document retrieval.

00:17:47.466 --> 00:17:50.069
The results show that MUVERA achieves its promise.

00:17:50.669 --> 00:17:52.571
Significantly faster search.

00:17:52.905 --> 00:17:57.109
while maintaining high precision compared to the original ColPali multi-vectors.

00:17:57.709 --> 00:18:02.114
The compressed fixed dimensional encodings enable HNSW indexing,

00:18:02.514 --> 00:18:04.716
dramatically improving query latency.

00:18:05.717 --> 00:18:08.887
The two-stage retrieval pattern is the recommended production approach.

00:18:09.188 --> 00:18:11.790
Use MUVERA's speed for initial candidate retrieval.

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:15.394
then leverage ColPali's accuracy for final reranking.

00:18:16.195 --> 00:18:19.898
This hybrid strategy delivers both performance and quality,

00:18:20.199 --> 00:18:23.802
making multi-vector models practical for large-scale applications.

00:18:25.104 --> 00:18:26.672
In the real-world scenarios,

00:18:26.972 --> 00:18:29.174
with thousands or millions of documents,

00:18:29.575 --> 00:18:32.478
MUVERA's benefits become even more pronounced.
